{#
  T-4.06a — the shoppable image primitive.

  ONE COMPONENT, TWO PRESENTATIONS. The owner's ruling of 2026-08-06 makes this
  mandatory rather than merely economical: **one hotspot-and-pill component serves
  T-4.06 and T-7.07, and a second implementation is a defect.** The artboards show
  the same marker and the same product in three places; what differs is where the
  pills sit — over the image as white cards (`overlay`), or beneath it as flat
  rows between rules (`list`, which hotspot.scss draws differently for that
  reason).

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.ui.hotspot-image' with {
        image:     component.image,
        alt:       component.alt,
        caption:   component.caption,
        layout:    component.layout,
        hotspots:  component.hotspots,
        id:        'lookbook-' ~ position,
        indicator: true
    } %}

  Parameters:
    image     required — the editorial photograph
    alt       optional — empty means decorative, see below
    caption   optional — the tilted label over the image's top corner, which the
              mobile Home artboard draws on its first block («The new Stanley 1913
              x JENNIE Collection is here.»). Merchant copy; empty renders nothing.
              The story modal never passes it.
    layout    'overlay' (pills over the image) or 'list' (pills beneath it)
    hotspots  a list of { x, y, product_id }
    id        unique per instance; `aria-controls` needs it
    indicator true → a segmented position indicator under the pills, which only
              the Home lookbook's desktop column shows (home.js drives it). The
              story modal leaves it off.
    defer     true → this instance is NOT resolved at page load. Added in T-7.07:
              a feed of story modals holds one of these per story, and booting
              them all would fire a product lookup per story on a page where none
              of them is visible yet. The caller mounts it when it opens.

  COORDINATES ARE PERCENTAGES, AND A PIXEL VALUE ANYWHERE IS A DEFECT. That is
  the ruling and it is also the only thing that works: the image is fluid, so a
  point fixed in pixels drifts off its product the moment the viewport changes.
  They arrive as numbers and are written as `--x`/`--y` custom properties with a
  literal `%` appended here, so no stylesheet has to remember.

  THE PILLS ARE RENDERED BY JAVASCRIPT, AND THE MARKUP HERE IS DELIBERATELY
  EMPTY OF PRODUCT DATA. The customiser has no product picker — the merchant
  types an id — and Twig cannot resolve an id to a product. `salla.product.getDetails()`
  can, at runtime, which is what makes the acceptance criterion true: **a renamed
  or repriced product needs no edit here**, because nothing about it is stored.

  THE NON-VISUAL EQUIVALENT IS THE PILL LIST ITSELF. The criterion asks for one,
  and the artboard already draws it: every hotspot has a pill, every pill names
  its product and its price, and the pills are in the DOM whether or not anyone
  presses a marker. The markers are a shortcut to them, not the only route.
#}
{% set hotspot_layout = layout|default('overlay') %}
{% set hotspot_id = id|default('hotspot-image') %}
{#
  The product each point names, resolved once for both loops below.

  A point picks its product through the customiser's product picker
  (`point.product`, an `items` field with `source: products`). The picker's
  value arrives as a list even with one choice allowed, and an entry may be the
  product itself or only its id — both are handled. A point saved before the
  picker existed carries a typed `point.product_id`; that is the fallback, so
  no merchant's existing hotspots go blank.

  Resolved here rather than in an included partial: a variable set inside an
  `include` does not come back out to the including template.
#}
{% set hotspot_product_ids = [] %}
{% for point in hotspots %}
    {% set point_product = point.product is defined and point.product ? point.product : null %}
    {% if point_product is iterable %}
        {% set point_product = point_product|first %}
    {% endif %}
    {% if point_product %}
        {% set point_product_id = point_product.id is defined ? point_product.id : point_product %}
    {% else %}
        {% set point_product_id = point.product_id|default('') %}
    {% endif %}
    {% set hotspot_product_ids = hotspot_product_ids|merge([point_product_id]) %}
{% endfor %}

<div class="hotspot hotspot--{{ hotspot_layout }}"
     data-hotspot-root
     {% if defer|default(false) %}data-hotspot-defer{% endif %}>
    <figure class="hotspot__figure">
        <img class="hotspot__image"
             src="{{ image|cdn(800) }}"
         srcset="{{ image|cdn(400) }} 400w, {{ image|cdn(800) }} 800w, {{ image|cdn(1200) }} 1200w"
         sizes="(min-width: 1536px) 1280px, calc(100vw - 32px)"
             alt="{{ alt|default('') }}"
             width="900"
             height="1200"
             loading="lazy"
             decoding="async">

        {% for point in hotspots %}
            {% set point_id = hotspot_id ~ '-' ~ loop.index %}
            <button type="button"
                    class="hotspot__marker"
                    style="--x: {{ point.x }}%; --y: {{ point.y }}%"
                    data-hotspot-marker
                    data-product-id="{{ hotspot_product_ids[loop.index0] }}"
                    aria-controls="{{ point_id }}"
                    aria-label="{{ trans('theme.hotspot.marker') }}">
                <span class="hotspot__marker-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </button>
        {% endfor %}

        {# A vector label laid over the photograph, not part of it (`Home Page (No
           Scroll).svg`, rect x219 y1247.6 rotate(16)). `dir="auto"` because the
           drawn copy is Latin inside an RTL page — on the inner span, not the
           label: logical insets resolve against the element's own direction,
           so an auto-LTR label would jump to the other corner. #}
        {% if caption|default('') %}
            <p class="hotspot__caption"><span dir="auto">{{ caption }}</span></p>
        {% endif %}
    </figure>

    {# The column wraps the pills so the desktop lookbook can hang its indicator
       under the scrolling row without the indicator scrolling with it. On
       mobile it is a plain block and changes nothing. #}
    <div class="hotspot__column">
        <ul class="hotspot__pills" data-carousel-track>
            {% for point in hotspots %}
                <li class="hotspot__pill"
                    id="{{ hotspot_id ~ '-' ~ loop.index }}"
                    data-hotspot-pill
                    data-product-id="{{ hotspot_product_ids[loop.index0] }}"
                    hidden>
                    {# Filled by partials/hotspots.js from the product id. Hidden until
                       then, so a failed lookup leaves no empty card behind. #}
                </li>
            {% endfor %}
        </ul>

        {% if indicator|default(false) %}
            <div class="product-carousel__indicator product-carousel__indicator--segments hotspot__indicator"
                 data-carousel-indicator="segments"
                 aria-hidden="true"
                 hidden></div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
</div>
